      "text": "Let's begin solving the given complex fraction, $\\dfrac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}$, where $x$ and $y$ are positive real numbers. We have a pretty straightforward expression. So, we can check our intuition before diving into prolonged calculation. If I ever had half the calculation skills than I do today, I am confident I would come up with a quicker calculation.\n\nTo find the maximum value\n\nresulting from\n\n1) expand the numerator of\n\n2/4,\n\n2) expand the denominator, and\n\n3) split the numerator into two isolated fractions.\n\nThus,\n\n$\\begin{aligned} \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} & =\\frac{\\text{Squeezed under parentheses}}{\\text{Outside denominator}}.\\\\[6pt]\n\n& =\\frac{\\text{Squeezed under parentheses}_1 + \\text{Squeezed under parentheses}_2}{\\text{Outside denominator}}.\\\\[6pt]\n\n& =\\frac{\\text{Start of Squeeze}_1 + \\text{Start of Squeeze}_2 + \\text{ Start of Squeeze}_1 + \\text{Start of Squeeze}_2}{\\text{Outside denominator}}.\\\\[6pt]\n\n\\end{aligned}$\n\n4) Done?\n\nWith each organized complex fraction with a numerator slightly larger than the denominator, cower, continue expanding, and apply an organization technique in each step.\n\nRemember that your aim is to discover the source of the results. So don't leave any \"half-explained\" under the integral.  \n\n5) There might be signs along the way, but they cannot lead us to the correct conclusion in the end. Prolonged work leads to purely frenzy, therefore, slashed, long is less effective, therefore, faster\n\n6) Simplify numerator and denominator \n\nEat some chocolates for maybe a minute. Then, everybody shakes their heads when they hear the solution for 2/2.\n\n\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{2}$. Their attempt got all the way to 2/4 (I think), but the division is uninteresting.",
