      "text": "We want to maximize the function $f(x,y) = \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}$.\nBy expanding and simplifying, we have\n\\[f(x,y) = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = 1 + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}.\\]\nTo maximize $f(x,y)$, we want to maximize $\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}$.\nThis expression is maximized when $2xy = x^2 + y^2$, which occurs when $x = y$.\nSubstituting $x = y$ into $f(x,y)$ gives\n\\[f(x,x) = 1 + \\frac{2x^2}{2x^2} = 1 + 1 = 2.\\]\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\frac{(x+y)^2}{x^2+y^2}$ is $\\boxed{2}$.\nThe answer is: 2",
