      "text": "We use the Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality to find this expression's maximum value.  We get\n\\[(x + y)^2 \\le \\frac{(x^2 + y^2)(1^2 + 1^2)}{2} \\iff \\left(x + \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^2 \\le x^2 + y^2 \\iff x \\le y \\sin \\theta \\le 1,\\]\nwhere $\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\approx 45^{\\circ}$.  Taking $\\sin$ of the other side gives\n\\begin{align*}\nx + y^{\\prime} \\lea & \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n& \\sin \\arcsin \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) + \\sin^{-1} \\left(\\frac{y{ }}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) \\\\\n& \\sin(\\arcsin(a) + \\sin^{-1}(b)) \\\\\n& \\sin(a + b) \\\\\n&  \\sin 45^{\\circ} = a \\sin b + b \\sin a \\\\\n\\sin \\arcsin \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right) & \\sin y + \\sin(45^{\\circ} - y) \\\\\ny \\cos 45^{\\circ} - \\sin y \\cos 45^{\\circ} + \\sin y + \\sin 45^{\\circ} \\cos y - \\cos y \\sin 45^{\\circ} \\\\\n\\sqrt{2} y \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - y \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + y + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nWhich simplifies to\n\\[y \\le x + y \\sin (45^{\\circ} - y) + \\sin (45^{\\circ} - y).\\]\nWe see that for sufficiently large $y$, we want $y - \\sin (45^{\\circ} - y) \\ge 0$, which is less than $2 \\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}} = x + y$, which is a contradiction, so the maximum occurs when $y \\approx x$, giving\n\\[2 \\cdot x \\sin 45^{\\circ} = \\boxed{2 \\sqrt{2}}.\\]\n\nWow wow that was detailed and tedious! taking it one step further, let's see what if we actually use the mean value triple integral. I can't believe I actually wrote this out, but welcome in. it happens to me very often. let's see where we are\n\n**edit** don't give it up, I'm trying  :jump:\n\nIf $f$ is a repulsive function, like $u^2$ on $D=\\{x^2+y^2<1\\}$\n\nUse polar coordinates $x=r\\cos c, y=r \\sin c$. integrate $f$ over $D$...\n\nMaybe after I clean up, I can try 2D/Cartesian...\n\n<P>edit</P> hello, also you can use the mean value triple integral.\r\nSonnhard.\n\n<P>ed